2026-06-30 15:26:58,371 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-d4b53bb4-3415-4dbe-9131-686bf3c7b569', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert proteomics assistant. Return valid JSON only.'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': 'You are a proteomics expert analyzing crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nYour task is to cluster raw spectral files into biologically meaningful groups.\n\nA cluster should represent a unique combination of:\n1. Biological / experimental condition\n   - Examples: treatment, control, carbon source, growth medium, cell state, tissue, disease state, time point, perturbation, purified complex, recombinant standard set.\n2. Source organism of the proteome being analyzed\n   - Return the full scientific name.\n   - Use the organism of the biological sample or proteome of interest, not necessarily the expression host.\n   - Example: if human proteins are expressed in E. coli and analyzed as human recombinant standards, the source organism is Homo sapiens.\n   - If the dataset intentionally contains multiple source organisms, create separate clusters by organism when possible.\n3. Crosslinking reagent\n   - Examples: BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU, formaldehyde.\n   - If multiple crosslinkers are used, create separate clusters by reagent when possible.\n\nDo NOT split clusters by:\n- fraction number\n- LC fraction\n- technical replicate\n- biological replicate ID\n- injection number\n- file numbering\n- enrichment fraction\n- instrument run order\n- search batch\n- calibration/QC files\n\nReplicates, fractions, and repeated injections from the same biological condition should be grouped together.\n\n---\n\nINPUTS\n\nRaw filenames:\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_100_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_100_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_101_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_101_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_102_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_102_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_104_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_104_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_105_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_105_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_106_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_106_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_107_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_107_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_108_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_108_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_109_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_109_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_112_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_112_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_128_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_128_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_129_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_129_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_196_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_196_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_79_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_79_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_82_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_82_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_83_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_83_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_91_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_91_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_92_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_92_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_93_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_93_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_94_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_94_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_95_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_95_2.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_96_1.raw\n20210625_Rix_Yi_Exclusion_FBDD_96_2.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run1_K1.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run1_K2.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run1_K5.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run1_K6.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run2_K1.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run2_K2.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run2_K5.raw\n20211207_Rix_Yi_SuTex_run2_K6.raw\n\nPublication text and PRIDE metadata:\n[TITLE]\nChemical proteomics with novel fully functionalized fragments and stringent target prioritization identifies the glutathione-dependent isomerase GSTZ1 as a lung cancer target\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nPhotoreactive fragment-like probes have been applied to discover target proteins that constitute novel cellular vulnerabilities and to identify viable chemical hits for drug discovery. Through forming covalent bonds, functionalized probes can achieve stronger target engagement and require less effort for on-target mechanism validation. However, the design of probe libraries, which directly affects the biological target space that is interrogated, and effective target prioritization remain critical challenges of such a chemical proteomic platform. In this study, we designed and synthesized a diverse panel of twenty fragment-based probes with privileged structural motifs containing both natural product and lead-like elements. These probes were fully functionalized with orthogonal diazirine and alkyne moieties and used for protein crosslinking in live lung cancer cells, target enrichment via “click chemistry,” and subsequent target identification through label-free quantitative LC-MS/MS analysis. Pair-wise comparison with a blunted negative control probe and stringent prioritization via individual cross-comparisons against the entire panel identified glutathione S-transferase zeta 1 (GSTZ1) as a specific and unique target candidate. DepMap database query, RNA interference-based gene silencing and proteome-wide tyrosine reactivity profiling suggested that GSTZ1 cooperated with different oncogenic alterations by supporting survival signaling in refractory NSCLC cells. This finding may form the basis for developing novel GSTZ1 inhibitors to improve the therapeutic efficacy of oncogene-directed targeted drugs. In summary, we designed a novel fragment-based probe panel and developed a target prioritization scheme with improved stringency, which allows for identification of unique target candidates, such as GSTZ1 in refractory lung cancer.\n\n[METHODS]\nMS-based analysis of crosslinked proteins Protein pellets were transferred to 5 mL microcentrifuge tubes and then resuspended in a freshly-prepared solution of proteomics-grade urea (320 μL, 6 M Urea in DPBS) containing 80 μL of 10% SDS and dissolved by sonication. To each solution, 4 mL DPBS was added (final conc: 0.2%, total volume) and incubated with pre-equilibrated NeutrAvidin agarose resin (100 μL 1:1 slurry, Pierce, Cat. 29201) for 2 hrs at ambient temperature on a rotator. The NeutrAvidin beads were collected by centrifugation (1,400 x g, 2 min) and sequentially washed with 0.2% SDS in DPBS (2 x 2 mL), detergent-free DPBS (2 x 2 mL), and 50 mM ammonium bicarbonate (AMBIC, 2 x 2 mL). Beads were resuspended with 200 μL 50 mM AMBIC and incubated with TCEP 50 μL (100 mM in 50 mM AMBIC) for 30 min at 37 oC. Iodoacetamide (50 μL of 200 mM in 50 mM AMBIC) was added and incubated for 30 min at ambient temperature. The beads were again washed with 2 x 1mL 50 mM AMBIC and resuspended in 100 μL of fresh 50 mM AMBIC and treated with 2 μg of sequencing grade modified trypsin (Promega, #V5111) at 37 °C overnight. These samples were acidified with formic acid (final conc: 5%) and centrifuged (1,000 x g, 2 min). The supernatant was collected and desalted through ZIPTIPs, and the desalted peptide mixture were dried in vacuum and reconstituted with HPLC buffer (2% acetonitrile, 0.1% formic acid) for LC-MS/MS analysis. Tryptic peptides of avidin were excluded from MS2 analysis to maximize detection of the protein targets. Samples were analyzed using LC-MS/MS after being spiked with 50 fmol/μL PRTC standards (88320, Pierce Retention Time Calibration Mixture, Thermo Fisher, MA). A nanoflow ultra-high performance liquid chromatograph (RSLC, Dionex, Sunnyvale, CA) and an electrospray bench top orbitrap mass spectrometer (Q-Exactive HFX, Thermo, San Jose, CA) were used for tandem mass spectrometry peptide sequencing experiments. The sample was first loaded onto a pre-column (C18 PepMap100 with 100 µm ID x 2 cm length packed with C18 reversed-phase resin, 5 µm particle size, 100 Å pore size) and washed for 8 minutes with aqueous 2% acetonitrile and 0.1% formic acid. The trapped peptides were eluted onto the analytical column, (C18 PepMap100 75 µm ID x 25 cm length, 2 µm particle size, 100 Å pore size, Thermo, San Jose, CA). The 120-minute gradient was programmed as: 95% solvent A (aqueous 2% acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid) for 8 minutes, solvent B (aqueous 90% acetonitrile + 0.1% formic acid) from 5% to 38.5% in 90 minutes, then solvent B from 50% to 90% B in 7 minutes and held at 90% for 5 minutes, followed by solvent B from 90% to 5% in 1 minute and re-equilibration for 10 minutes. The flow rate on the analytical column was 300 nL/min. nanospray voltage was 1900 V with capillary temperature at 275 °C. Twenty tandem mass spectra were collected in a data-dependent manner following each survey scan. The resolution settings were 60,000 and 45,000 for MS1 and MS/MS, respectively. Protein tyrosine reactivity profiling H1792 cells were treated with DMSO and sotorasib for 24 h. Cell lysates were prepared and diluted to 2 mg/mL in PBS. Samples were treated with HHS-482 (a SuTEx probe) at 25 µM for 1 h at room temperature. Labeled proteomes were subjected to CuAAC conjugation with Biotin-Azide conjugate (200 µM) by mixing with TCEP (1 mM), TBTA (100 µM), and CuSO4 (1 mM). After 1 h incubation, resulting mixtures were subjected to MeOH:CHCl3 extraction as described above. Protein discs were reconstituted with urea buffer supplemented with 10% SDS. Samples were processed with NeutrAvidin beads, on-bead digestion with trypsin, and ZIPTIP purification as described above. Samples were dried in vacuum and prepared for LC-MS/MS analysis.  Sample naming information: Duplicate fragment-based drug discovery samples coded by probe (P):  Experiment 79 (P10), 82 (P11), 83 (P19), 91 (P8), 92 (P15), 93 (P13), 94 (P9), 95 (P18), 96 (P12), 100 (P14), 101 (P17), 102 (P20), 104 (P16), 105 (P6), 106 (P7), 107 (P5), 108 (P4), 109 (P2), 112 (P21), 128 (P1), 129 (P3), and 196 (P22).  The SuTex samples are untreated (K1/K5) or sotorasib-treated (K2/K6); K1 and K2 are the first bioreplicate, while K5 and K6 are the second bioreplicate; all samples analyzed twice.\n\n\nPreviously extracted organism(s) and NCBI taxid(s):\nHomo sapiens (human)\n\n---\n\nTASKS\n\n1. Identify the distinct biological/experimental conditions described by the publication, PRIDE metadata, and filenames.\n\n2. Assign every raw file to exactly one cluster.\n\n3. Define clusters using the smallest number of biologically meaningful groups. Do not over-cluster based only on replicate, fraction, or run-number differences.\n\n4. For each cluster, report:\n   - cluster_id: "cluster_1", "cluster_2", etc.\n   - cluster_label: short machine-readable label using lowercase words separated by underscores\n   - description: concise human-readable summary of the condition\n   - organism: full scientific name of the proteome/sample organism\n   - taxid: NCBI Taxonomy CURIE, e.g. "NCBITaxon:9606"; use null if unknown\n   - crosslinker: crosslinking reagent; use null if unknown\n   - biological_condition: short phrase describing the treatment/control/state\n   - files: list of raw filenames assigned to this cluster\n   - confidence: "high", "medium", or "low"\n   - evidence_quote: exact sentence or short phrase from the publication, PRIDE metadata, or filename pattern that supports this cluster assignment\n   - reasoning: brief explanation of why these files belong together\n\n5. Assign files that cannot be confidently interpreted to cluster_0 with:\n   - cluster_label: "unassigned"\n   - confidence: "low"\n   - evidence_quote: null\n   - reasoning: explain why assignment was not possible\n\n6. QC, calibrant, blank, wash, or test files should be assigned to clus